<h2>Where the remedy comes from</h2>
<p>Two provisions combine. Section 34 of the Specific Relief Act, 1963 lets a person entitled to a legal character — and marital status is the classic legal character — sue for a declaration against anyone denying or interested in denying it. Section 7(1) of the Family Courts Act, 1984, through its Explanation, expressly assigns to Family Courts the suits and proceedings for &ldquo;a declaration as to the validity of a marriage or as to the matrimonial status of any person&rdquo;. The result: in Delhi, both the person asserting a marriage and the person denying one sue in the Family Court of the appropriate district, and the decree binds as any civil decree does.</p>

<h2>The typical scenarios</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The fabricated marriage.</strong> A forged certificate or a staged record surfaces — often discovered through a maintenance notice, a visa inquiry or a property claim. The victim sues for a declaration that no marriage exists between the parties, and for consequential cancellation of the offending record.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The denied marriage.</strong> A spouse — commonly a woman married in a small or informal ceremony — is disowned by the family after the husband&rsquo;s death. She sues for a declaration that she is the widow, as the foundation for succession, pension and residence rights.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The disputed second marriage.</strong> Whether a second marriage was solemnised at all determines exposure in bigamy allegations and the competing families&rsquo; civil rights; a status declaration frames the question cleanly.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The uncertain divorce.</strong> Where a customary or foreign divorce is asserted by one side and denied by the other, the present matrimonial status itself may need declaration before either party can safely remarry or litigate further.</div>
</div>

<h2>Proof: ceremony, record, conduct</h2>
<p>Because Hindu and most personal-law marriages are constituted by ceremony, the fact in issue is usually whether the essential ceremonies took place. Evidence falls into three bands. First, the record: the certificate and the registration file behind it — which, in forgery cases, is itself the battlefield, examined through the registrar&rsquo;s record, signatures and supporting documents. Second, the ceremony: photographs, video, the officiating priest, the venue&rsquo;s records, invitation cards, and attendees. Third, conduct: joint residence, hotel and travel records, documents in which either party described the other as spouse, and the way the couple presented themselves socially. Sustained cohabitation and holding-out can ground a presumption in favour of marriage, so the side denying a marriage should assemble its case early and completely.</p>

<h2>Strategy notes</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Act on discovery. A person who learns of a false certificate should send a notice, lodge the appropriate criminal complaint regarding the forgery, and file the declaration suit — delay reads as acquiescence and lets the false record contaminate more registries.</p>
  <p>Frame consequential reliefs with the declaration: cancellation of the forged instrument and injunctions against its use. A bare declaration without consequential relief can be objected to under the proviso to Section 34 of the Specific Relief Act, which bars a declaration where further relief could be, but is not, sought.</p>
  <p>Coordinate parallel proceedings. Maintenance claims under Section 144 BNSS can proceed on a prima facie view of marriage; a pending status suit does not automatically stall them, which makes speed in the declaration suit itself the real protection.</p>
  <p>Remember limitation: declaratory claims are governed by the Limitation Act&rsquo;s residuary three-year period running from when the right to sue accrues — practically, from the clear denial or the discovery of the false assertion.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer describes the general framework for status declarations. Individual cases turn on personal law, the ceremony alleged and the documents in play; nothing here is legal advice for a specific dispute.</div>
